{"id": "rp3-00000", "q": "What is 41 times 15?", "a": "To calculate $41 \\times 15$, you can break the problem down using the distributive property or standard multiplication:\n\n**Method 1: Breaking it down**\n$$41 \\times 15 = 41 \\times (10 + 5)$$\n1.  $41 \\times 10 = 410$\n2.  $41 \\times 5 = 205$\n3.  Add the results: $410 + 205 = 615$\n\n**Method 2: Standard Multiplication**\n```text\n    41\n  x 15\n  -----\n   205  (41 * 5)\n   410  (41 * 10)\n  -----\n   615\n```\n\n**Answer:**\n41 times 15 is **615**."}
{"id": "rp3-00001", "q": "Add 90 and 47.", "a": "To add 90 and 47, you can break it down by place value:\n\n1.  Add the tens: $90 + 40 = 130$\n2.  Add the ones: $0 + 7 = 7$\n3.  Combine them: $130 + 7 = 137$\n\nAlternatively, you can stack them vertically:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c@{\\,}c@{\\,}c}\n & 9 & 0 \\\\\n+ & 4 & 7 \\\\\n\\hline\n & 1 & 3 & 7 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe sum is **137**."}
{"id": "rp3-00002", "q": "Summarise in one sentence: the pump was restarted at 6 am and the tank refilled by 7 am.", "a": "The pump was restarted at 6 am, resulting in the tank being refilled by 7 am."}
{"id": "rp3-00003", "q": "If a tank fills 95 litres per minute, how long for 8 litres?", "a": "To find out how long it takes to fill 8 litres, we can use the following steps:\n\n### Step-by-Step Deduction\n\n1.  **Identify the given rates**:\n    *   Flow rate: $95$ litres per minute.\n    *   Target volume: $8$ litres.\n\n2.  **Set up the calculation**:\n    Time is calculated by dividing the total volume needed by the flow rate:\n    $$ \\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Volume}}{\\text{Rate}} $$\n    $$ \\text{Time} = \\frac{8}{95} \\text{ minutes} $$\n\n3.  **Perform the division**:\n    $$ 8 \\div 95 \\approx 0.08421 \\text{ minutes} $$\n\n4.  **Convert to seconds** (for better readability):\n    Since there are $60$ seconds in a minute, multiply the decimal minutes by $60$:\n    $$ 0.08421 \\times 60 \\approx 5.0526 \\text{ seconds} $$\n\n### Final Conclusion\n\nIt will take approximately **0.084 minutes** or **5.05 seconds** to fill 8 litres."}
{"id": "rp3-00004", "q": "Add 97 and 15.", "a": "To add 97 and 15, you can break it down step-by-step:\n\n1.  Start with **97**.\n2.  Add **15** to it.\n    *   First, add the ones place: $7 + 5 = 12$. Write down 2 and carry over 1.\n    *   Next, add the tens place along with the carried 1: $9 + 1 + 1 = 11$.\n3.  Combine the results: **112**.\n\nAlternatively, you can think of it as:\n$$97 + 15 = 97 + 3 + 12 = 100 + 12 = 112$$\n\nThe sum is **112**."}
{"id": "rp3-00005", "q": "Add 61 and 80.", "a": "To add 61 and 80, you can break it down simply:\n\n1.  Add the ones place: $1 + 0 = 1$\n2.  Add the tens place: $60 + 80 = 140$\n3.  Combine them: $140 + 1 = 141$\n\n**61 + 80 = 141**"}
{"id": "rp3-00006", "q": "What does TDS in drinking water mean? Keep it short.", "a": "**TDS** stands for **Total Dissolved Solids**. It refers to the total amount of microscopic particles dissolved in your water, such as minerals (calcium, magnesium), salts, and metals.\n\nIt is measured in parts per million (ppm) and indicates water purity:\n*   **Low TDS (0–50 ppm)**: Very pure water.\n*   **Medium TDS (50–500 ppm)**: Good quality drinking water.\n*   **High TDS (>500 ppm)**: May indicate high mineral content or potential contamination."}
{"id": "rp3-00007", "q": "What is 97 times 71?", "a": "To calculate $97 \\times 71$, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm or the distributive property.\n\n**Method 1: Standard Multiplication**\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\quad}l}\n  97 & \\\\\n\\times 71 & \\\\\n\\hline\n  97 & \\quad (\\text{This is } 97 \\times 1) \\\\\n6590 & \\quad (\\text{This is } 97 \\times 70) \\\\\n\\hline\n6887 & \\\\\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\n*   Step 1: Multiply 97 by 1 to get **97**.\n*   Step 2: Multiply 97 by 70.\n    *   $97 \\times 7 = 679$\n    *   Add the zero: **6790**\n*   Step 3: Add the results ($97 + 6790$).\n    *   $6790 + 90 = 6880$\n    *   $6880 + 7 = 6887$\n\n**Method 2: Distributive Property**\n\nWe can rewrite 97 as $(100 - 3)$:\n$$97 \\times 71 = (1"}
{"id": "rp3-00008", "q": "What is 79 times 20?", "a": "To calculate $79 \\times 20$, you can break it down into simpler steps:\n\n1.  First, multiply $79$ by $2$:\n    $$79 \\times 2 = 158$$\n\n2.  Since the original number was $20$ (which is $2 \\times 10$), you then multiply the result by $10$:\n    $$158 \\times 10 = 1580$$\n\nAlternatively, you can think of it as adding $79$ to itself $20$ times, or simply shifting the decimal point one place to the right after multiplying $79 \\times 2$.\n\n**Answer:**\n$$79 \\times 20 = 1580$$"}
{"id": "rp3-00009", "q": "Explain a capacitor bank in two sentences for a new facilities operator.", "a": "A capacitor bank is a large electrical device that stores energy temporarily to improve the efficiency of your power system by reducing the amount of reactive power your facility draws from the grid. By installing this equipment, you can lower your electricity bills, reduce strain on your transformers, and prevent potential equipment failures caused by poor power quality."}
{"id": "rp3-00010", "q": "If a tank fills 92 litres per minute, how long for 59 litres?", "a": "To find out how long it takes to fill 59 litres, we can use the formula for time based on rate and volume:\n\n$$ \\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Volume}}{\\text{Rate}} $$\n\n**Step-by-step Calculation:**\n\n1.  **Identify the given values:**\n    *   Rate ($R$) = $92$ litres/minute\n    *   Volume ($V$) = $59$ litres\n\n2.  **Substitute the values into the formula:**\n    $$ T = \\frac{59}{92} $$\n\n3.  **Perform the division:**\n    $$ T \\approx 0.6413 $$\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nIt will take approximately **0.64 minutes** (or about 38.5 seconds) to fill 59 litres."}
{"id": "rp3-00011", "q": "Why does a capacitor bank matter on a university campus?", "a": "A capacitor bank is a critical piece of infrastructure on a university campus because it acts as an **energy stabilizer and efficiency booster** for the entire electrical grid. Universities are unique environments with massive, fluctuating energy demands, making them ideal candidates for this technology.\n\nHere is a detailed breakdown of why a capacitor bank matters in this specific context:\n\n### 1. Correction of Power Factor\nThis is the primary technical reason. Most university equipment (computers, lighting, HVAC, labs) uses induction motors or electronic power supplies that draw current out of phase with the voltage. This creates a condition called a **low power factor**.\n*   **The Problem:** A low power factor means the utility company has to supply more current than necessary to do the actual work, leading to wasted energy and increased costs for both the university and the power provider. It also causes voltage drops across long campus wiring.\n*   **The Solution:** A capacitor bank stores reactive power locally. By injecting this reactive power back into the grid, it cancels out the lagging current from the motors. This improves the **power factor** (bringing it closer to 1.0), ensuring that the electricity flowing through the wires is actually doing useful work rather than just circulating.\n\n### 2. Cost Reduction and Incentives\nUniversities operate on tight budgets, and energy costs are a significant operational expense.\n*   **Lower Utility Bills:** By improving the power factor, the university reduces the amount"}
{"id": "rp3-00012", "q": "If a tank fills 28 litres per minute, how long for 23 litres?", "a": "To find out how long it takes to fill 23 litres, we can use the formula for time:\n\n$$ \\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Volume}}{\\text{Rate}} $$\n\n**Given:**\n*   **Rate:** 28 litres per minute\n*   **Volume:** 23 litres\n\n**Calculation:**\n$$ \\text{Time} = \\frac{23}{28} \\text{ minutes} $$\n\n$$ \\text{Time} \\approx 0.8214 \\text{ minutes} $$\n\nTo make this more understandable, let's convert the decimal part into seconds:\n1.  Take the decimal part: $0.8214$\n2.  Multiply by 60 to get seconds: $0.8214 \\times 60 \\approx 49.29$ seconds\n\n**Conclusion:**\nIt will take approximately **0.82 minutes** (or about **49 seconds**) to fill 23 litres."}
{"id": "rp3-00013", "q": "If a tank fills 77 litres per minute, how long for 35 litres?", "a": "To find out how long it takes to fill 35 litres, we can use the formula:\n\n$$ \\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Volume}}{\\text{Rate}} $$\n\n**Step-by-step Calculation:**\n\n1.  **Identify the given values:**\n    *   Rate of filling = $77$ litres per minute\n    *   Target volume = $35$ litres\n\n2.  **Set up the equation:**\n    $$ \\text{Time} = \\frac{35}{77} \\text{ minutes} $$\n\n3.  **Simplify the fraction:**\n    Both numbers are divisible by $7$:\n    *   $35 \\div 7 = 5$\n    *   $77 \\div 7 = 11$\n    \n    So, the time is $\\frac{5}{11}$ of a minute.\n\n4.  **Convert to seconds (optional but helpful):**\n    Since there are $60$ seconds in a minute:\n    $$ \\frac{5}{11} \\times 60 = \\frac{300}{11} \\approx 27.27 \\text{ seconds} $$\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nIt will take **$\\frac{5}{11}$ of a minute** (or approximately **27.3 seconds**) to fill 35 litres."}
{"id": "rp3-00014", "q": "If a tank fills 49 litres per minute, how long for 95 litres?", "a": "To find out how long it takes to fill 95 litres, we can use the formula for time:\n\n$$ \\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Total Volume}}{\\text{Rate}} $$\n\n**Step-by-step Calculation:**\n\n1.  **Identify the given values:**\n    *   Total Volume needed = $95$ litres\n    *   Filling Rate = $49$ litres per minute\n\n2.  **Set up the division:**\n    $$ \\text{Time} = \\frac{95}{49} $$\n\n3.  **Perform the division:**\n    *   $95 \\div 49 \\approx 1.93877...$\n\n4.  **Convert the decimal to minutes and seconds (optional but helpful):**\n    *   The whole number part is **1 minute**.\n    *   The remaining decimal is $0.93877...$ minutes.\n    *   Convert this to seconds: $0.93877... \\times 60 \\approx 56.33$ seconds.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nIt will take approximately **1.94 minutes** (or about **1 minute and 56 seconds**) to fill 95 litres."}
{"id": "rp3-00015", "q": "Convert 35 degrees Celsius to Fahrenheit.", "a": "To convert a temperature from degrees Celsius ($^\\circ\\text{C}$) to degrees Fahrenheit ($^\\circ\\text{F}$), we use the following formula:\n\n$$F = (C \\times \\frac{9}{5}) + 32$$\n\nHere is the step-by-step calculation for **35°C**:\n\n1.  **Multiply by 9/5 (or 1.8):**\n    $$35 \\times 1.8 = 63$$\n\n2.  **Add 32:**\n    $$63 + 32 = 95$$\n\n**Answer:**\n35 degrees Celsius is equal to **95 degrees Fahrenheit**."}
{"id": "rp3-00016", "q": "What is 98 times 25?", "a": "To calculate $98 \\times 25$, you can use a few different methods:\n\n**Method 1: Direct Multiplication**\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\quad}l}\n  98 & \\\\\n\\times 25 & \\\\\n\\hline\n 490 & (98 \\times 5) \\\\\n1960 & (98 \\times 20) \\\\\n\\hline\n2450 &\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\n**Method 2: Using the \"Round Number\" Trick**\nSince $98$ is very close to $100$, you can rewrite the problem as:\n$$98 \\times 25 = (100 - 2) \\times 25$$\nUsing the distributive property:\n$$= (100 \\times 25) - (2 \\times 25)$$\n$$= 2500 - 50$$\n$$= 2450$$\n\n**Answer:**\n98 times 25 is **2,450**."}
{"id": "rp3-00017", "q": "What should I check first if a room feels stuffy?", "a": "If a room feels stuffy, the most immediate and impactful thing to check is the **ventilation and air exchange**. Stuffy air is often caused by a lack of fresh oxygen and an excess of carbon dioxide, humidity, or pollutants.\n\nHere is a logical sequence of things to check, starting with the most common culprits:\n\n### 1. Check the Windows and Vents\n*   **Are they open?** Even a crack of an inch can significantly improve airflow. If windows are closed, try opening them slightly to let fresh air in.\n*   **Are the HVAC vents blocked?** Check if furniture, curtains, or rugs are blocking the supply or return vents. Ensure the thermostat is set to \"cool\" or \"heat\" (depending on the season) and that the fan is running.\n*   **Is the air filter clogged?** If your home has a central heating or cooling system, a dirty filter restricts airflow, making the air feel stagnant and dry or humid.\n\n### 2. Assess Humidity Levels\nStuffy often implies high humidity, which makes the air feel heavy and uncomfortable.\n*   **Check the humidity:** If you have a hygrometer, look at the number. If it's above **60%**, the air is likely too moist.\n*   **Solutions:** Run a **dehumidifier** or an **air conditioner** (ACs naturally dehumidify). If you don't"}
{"id": "rp3-00018", "q": "What is 82 times 68?", "a": "To calculate $82 \\times 68$, we can use the standard multiplication algorithm or the distributive property.\n\n**Method 1: Standard Multiplication**\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\quad}l}\n  82 & \\\\\n\\times 68 & \\\\\n\\hline\n 656 & (8 \\times 82) \\\\\n4920 & (60 \\times 82) \\\\\n\\hline\n5576 &\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\n*   $8 \\times 2 = 16$ (write 6, carry 1)\n*   $8 \\times 8 = 64$, plus 1 is 65.\n*   $6 \\times 2 = 12$ (write 2, carry 1)\n*   $6 \\times 8 = 48$, plus 1 is 49.\n*   Add the rows: $656 + 4920 = 5576$.\n\n**Method 2: Distributive Property**\n\nWe can break the numbers down:\n$$82 \\times 68 = (80 + 2) \\times (70 - 2)$$\nOr more simply:\n$$82 \\times 68 = 82 \\times (70 -"}
{"id": "rp3-00019", "q": "What is a common mistake people make about TDS in drinking water?", "a": "A very common mistake people make regarding Total Dissolved Solids (TDS) is **confusing it with \"badness\" or assuming that lower is always better.**\n\nMany individuals believe that drinking water with a low TDS count (e.g., below 100 ppm) is superior because they equate dissolved solids with impurities like bacteria, viruses, or heavy metals. However, TDS is simply a measurement of the total amount of dissolved minerals and salts in the water, not a measure of purity or safety regarding pathogens.\n\nHere is a breakdown of why this misconception is problematic:\n\n*   **Minerals are Essential**: Water with moderate TDS levels (typically between 150 ppm and 300 ppm) is rich in essential minerals such as calcium, magnesium, potassium, and sodium. These are vital for human health, helping with bone strength, muscle function, and nerve transmission.\n*   **The \"Low is Best\" Fallacy**: While water with extremely high TDS (often above 500–1000 ppm) can be unpalatable and may indicate high levels of undesirable salts or hardness, water with very low TDS (such as reverse osmosis water) is not necessarily \"cleaner\" in a biological sense. In fact, drinking only low-TDS water for long periods can lead to a deficiency in these essential minerals.\n*   **Safety vs. Taste**: TDS does not indicate the"}
{"id": "rp3-00020", "q": "What is 18 times 26?", "a": "To calculate $18 \\times 26$, we can break it down using the distributive property or standard multiplication:\n\n**Method 1: Standard Multiplication**\n$$\n\\begin{array}{r@{\\quad}l}\n  26 & \\\\\n\\times 18 & \\\\\n\\hline\n 208 & (8 \\times 26) \\\\\n260 & (10 \\times 26) \\\\\n\\hline\n468 &\n\\end{array}\n$$\n\n**Method 2: Breaking it down**\n$18 \\times 26 = (20 - 2) \\times 26$\n$= (20 \\times 26) - (2 \\times 26)$\n$= 520 - 52$\n$= 468$\n\n**Answer:**\n18 times 26 is **468**."}
{"id": "rp3-00021", "q": "If a tank fills 62 litres per minute, how long for 98 litres?", "a": "To find out how long it takes to fill 98 litres, we can use the formula for time:\n\n$$ \\text{Time} = \\frac{\\text{Total Volume}}{\\text{Rate}} $$\n\n**Step-by-step Calculation:**\n\n1.  **Identify the given values:**\n    *   Total Volume needed = $98$ litres\n    *   Filling Rate = $62$ litres per minute\n\n2.  **Set up the division:**\n    $$ \\text{Time} = \\frac{98}{62} $$\n\n3.  **Perform the division:**\n    $$ 98 \\div 62 \\approx 1.5806... $$\n\n4.  **Convert the decimal part to seconds (optional but helpful for clarity):**\n    *   The whole number is **1 minute**.\n    *   The decimal part is $0.5806$.\n    *   Convert to seconds: $0.5806 \\times 60 \\approx 34.8$ seconds.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nIt will take approximately **1.58 minutes** (or about **1 minute and 35 seconds**) to fill 98 litres."}
